WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
When heat reaches the bank: how a heatwave travels from a day’s wages to a lender’s books.

00:06.000 --> 00:14.000
A hotter day, a smaller wage. Heat cuts working hours. Incomes fall first for vendors, farmers and daily-wage workers.

00:14.000 --> 00:21.000
The instalment waits. Repayments slow, and field visits stop in the heat. In 2025, heatwaves cut what lenders collected in the June quarter.

00:21.000 --> 00:29.000
The strain moves up. Lenders face a cash crunch and costlier funding. About 60% of India’s ₹3.81 trillion microfinance portfolio is in farming and allied work.

00:29.000 --> 00:36.000
Regulators are moving. The Reserve Bank of India now treats heatwaves as a prudential risk. Disclosure of heat exposure is still voluntary for some.

00:36.000 --> 00:46.000
Three ways to pay for protection: parametric insurance that pays out when heat crosses a set line; blended finance, where patient capital takes early risk; and green and resilience bonds for resilient buildings, water and city infrastructure.

00:46.000 --> 00:52.000
And what makes it work: shared heat-risk data, lending tied to heat outcomes, and city-level platforms that pool many small projects.

00:52.000 --> 00:58.000
From financing vulnerability to financing resilience. Based on a commentary by Namita Vikas, Mongabay India, 29 June 2026. The views are the author’s.
